 ## Overview
-Delta tracking is a Monte Carlo method for sampling free path lengths in heterogeneous participating media by converting the medium into a homogeneous one via "fake" particles (null-scattering) [S1, S2, S4]. It solves the problem of computing transmittance and scattering events in volumes where the extinction coefficient varies spatially, avoiding the implicit bias of equidistant ray marching [S3, S4]. It sets itself apart from [Homogeneous Medium Sampling](980-homogeneous-medium-sampling.md) by handling spatially varying density without subdivision, and from [Ray Marching](1135-single-scattering-homogeneous-volume-rendering.md) by providing an unbiased estimator at the cost of potentially high variance in sparse regions [S3, S4].
+Delta tracking is a Monte Carlo method for sampling free path lengths in heterogeneous participating media by converting the medium into a homogeneous one via "fake" particles (null-scattering) [S1, S2, S4]. It solves the problem of computing transmittance and scattering events in volumes where the extinction coefficient varies spatially, avoiding the implicit bias of equidistant ray marching [S3, S4]. It sets itself apart from [Homogeneous Medium Sampling](980-homogeneous-medium-sampling.md) by handling spatially varying density without subdivision, and from [Single-scattering homogeneous volume rendering](1135-single-scattering-homogeneous-volume-rendering.md) by providing an unbiased estimator at the cost of potentially high variance in sparse regions [S3, S4].
 
